/*! \page developer_backend_own_page Ownership Hierarchy

\ref developer_backend_id_page     (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_build_page  (Next)    &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

Each executable program created by the PSCF package is designed 
using a hierarchical system of object ownership. All but one of the 
objects used in a PSCF program is a "child" that is owned by an instance 
of some higher-level "parent" class.  In this system, each "child" object 
is either a member variable of its parent, or a dynamically allocated 
object that is accessed through a pointer that is a member variable of 
the parent object. Each parent is responsible for creating and 
destroying its children.

The Pscf::Rp namespace contains a large number of definitions for class
templates that take the dimension of space, D, and a backend identifer 
class as template parameters. Throughout this namespace, the same
arguments for these two template parameters are used throughout objects 
the ownership hierarchy of objects used by a executable program.
An instance of a class template specialization with a specific pair 
of arguments for D and T may own children that are instances of 
other template specializations with the same values for these two
template arguments, and may be a child of a parent that is an instance 
of yet another class template specialization with the same arguments
of these parameters.

\section developer_backend_own_system_sec System class

The top of the ownership hierarchy used by the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg
programs is an instance of a specialization of the Pscf::Rp::System 
class template.  The top-level object used by the pscf_rpc program is 
an instance of a specialization of this template  with T=Pscf::CPT and 
D=1, 2, or 3, while the top-level object used by pscf_rpc program is 
an instance of a specialization with T=Pscf::CUT.  

A value for the integer D is passed to the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg programs 
as a command line parameter, as the parameter of command line option -d.  
Thus, for example, if the pscf_rpg program is invoked with a command line 
option "-d 3", as
\code
   pscf_rpg -d 3 -p param -c command
\endcode
then this program constructs an instance of the class 
\code
   Pscf::Rp::System \<3, CUT\>
\endcode
Member functions of this top-level System object are then invoked to 
read the parameter and command files, which are given in the above 
example as files named "param" and "command". 

\section developer_backend_own_children_sec Top-Level Children 

An instance of System \<D,T\> with a specific choice of values for the 
template parameters D and T directly or indirectly owns instances of 
many other class template specializations that all use the same 
arguments for these two template parameters. All of these objects in
the hierarchy of objects used by a particular executable are thereby
configured at compile time to use the same computational backend, and 
to treat systems with the same spatial dimension D.

The creation of separate template specializations for models with 
different values of D is used primarily for SCFT calculations, for which 
on might use D=1 to treat a lamellar phase or D=2 to treat a 2D hexagonal 
phase. PS-FTS simulations generally use a value D=3, in order to allow 
the fluctuating fields to depends on three spatial coordinates.

The direct children of an instance of System \<D,T\> include instances 
of the following class template specializations:
    - Pscf::Rp::Mixture \<D,T\>
    - Pscf::Rp::Domain \<D,T\>
    - Pscf::Rp::CFields \<D,T\>
    - Pscf::Rp::WFields \<D,T\>

Several of these top-level children are discussed separately below:

<b> Mixture </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::Mixture \<D,T\> owns a set of children that each 
solve the modified diffusion equation for particular molecular species. 
Among these are objects associated with particular block polymer species, 
which are instances of Pscf::Rp::Polymer \<D,T\>, and other objects 
associated with particular solvent species, which are instances of 
Pscf::Rp::Solvent \<D,T\>).  Each instance of Pscf::Rp::Polymer \<D,T\> 
has a set of child objects associated with different blocks within a block 
polymer, which are instances of Rp::Block \<D,T\>, as well as objects 
associated with propagators, which are instances of 
Pscf::Rp::Propagator \<D,T\>. Each propagator is associated with traversal 
of a block in one of two possible directions. 

<b> Domain </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::Domain \<D,T\> owns an instance of 
Pscf::Prdc::UnitCell \<D,T\> that contains information about the periodic 
simulation unit cell, and instance of Pscf::Mesh \<D\> that defines the
periodic mesh used for all numerical calculations. This "Domain" object 
also owns owns several other children that contain information about 
space group symmetries and symmetry adapted basis functions, which are 
used in SCFT calculations that search for solutions with a specified 
space group symmetry. 

<b> WFields </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::WFields \<D,T\> owns a set of objects that store 
chemical potential fields associated with different monomer types. 

<b> CFields </b>:
An instance of Pscf::Rp::CFields \<D,T\> owns a set of objects that store 
monomer concentration fields associated with different monomer types. 

<BR>
